==English==
[[File:Complete_Guide_to_Heraldry_Fig431.png|right|thumb|A '''cockatrice''' on a coat of arms.]]

===Etymology===
First attested 1382 as {{inh|en|enm|cocatrice|t=basilisk}}, from {{der|en|fro|cocatriz}}, from {{der|en|LL.|calcātrīx||she who treads upon something}}, from {{der|en|la|calcō||tread}}, from {{m|la|calx||heel, hoof}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒkətɹaɪs/|/ˈkɒkətɹɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|mythology}} A legendary creature about the size and shape of a [[dragon]] or [[wyvern]], but in appearance resembling a giant [[rooster]], with some lizard-like characteristics.
#: {{hyper|en|chimera}}
#: {{cot|en|basilisk|wyvern}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1910|author=Joseph Walker McSpadden|title=The Spell of Egypt
|passage=“Peace reigns in happy Luxor. The lion lies down with the lamb, and the child, if it will, may harmlessly put its hand into the '''cockatrice’s''' den.”}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[mistress|Mistress]], [[harlot]].
# {{lb|en|fantasy|folklore}} A snake or serpent that appears to be hatched from a rooster, or cock's, egg.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Jeremiah|8|17|passage=For, behold, I will send serpents, '''cockatrices''', among you, which will not be charmed, and they shall bite you, saith the {{smallcaps|Lord}}.}}
# {{lb|en|speculated}} The [[cobra]]. {{rfex|en}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|figurative}} Any [[venomous]] or [[deadly]] thing.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7|passage=this little '''cockatrice''' of a king}}

